def P(t): return "".join(f"<p>{x.strip()}</p>" for x in t.split("||"))
body = []
body.append('<h1>The Morning Letter</h1><div class="sub">With the compliments of the Editor, to Jonesboro, Brookland &amp; neighbors</div>')
body.append('<div class="date">Friday, the Twentieth of November, 2026</div><div class="orn">&#10086;</div>')
body.append('<p>Good morning,</p>')
body.append('<p>Two more summery days lie ahead, sunny and in the low 70s, before Sunday’s rain and a cold front open the door to the season’s first hard freeze by Wednesday, just in time to put Saturday’s duck opener and Zone 4’s last gun weekend in a fine light. In the news, a former deputy prosecutor is suing Prosecutor Sonia Hagood over her firing, the state reports on new mothers losing Medicaid, the Senate passes a sweeping energy-permitting bill, Russia sends a missile into Poland during a massive strike on Kyiv, and Anthropic’s record stock debut lands on a 1,100-point slide in the Dow.</p>')
body.append('<h2>The Day Ahead</h2>'); body.append(hourly); body.append(grid)
body.append(P('A mild start near 49 at six o’clock gives way to a cloudless, summery afternoon: about 58 by nine, 71 at noon and a high near 74 by three, with a light breeze and no rain in sight. The evening cools gently through the 60s after the 4:52 sunset, to the upper 50s by nine and about 50 by daybreak.||Saturday repeats the pattern, sunny and about 73, a fine day for the duck opener and the last weekend of Zone 4’s gun season. Sunday is the turn of the season: showers likely through the day, with rain chances above 80 percent by noon, and a cold front behind them that drops Sunday night to about 41. Monday clears but tops out near 55, and the cold deepens through Thanksgiving week, with lows near 33 Tuesday, 29 Wednesday morning, the first hard freeze of the season if it holds, and a sunny Thanksgiving Day near 50.'))
body.append('<h2>The Almanac</h2>')
body.append(f'''<table class="alm"><tr><td>First light</td><td>{alm['first_light']}</td><td>Sunrise</td><td>{alm['sunrise']}</td></tr>
<tr><td>Solar noon</td><td>{alm['noon']}</td><td>Sunset</td><td>{alm['sunset']}</td></tr>
<tr><td>Last light</td><td>{alm['last_light']}</td><td>Length of day</td><td>{alm['day_length']}</td></tr>
<tr><td>Change from yesterday</td><td>{alm['change'].replace('-','&minus;')}</td><td>Moon</td><td>{alm['moon']}, {alm['illum']}% lit</td></tr></table>''')
body.append(P('The sun is up at 6:44 and down at 4:52, a day of ten hours and eight minutes that is a minute and a half shorter than Thursday’s. The moon, three-quarters lit and still waxing, climbs the eastern sky through the afternoon and will be full around Tuesday, Nov. 24, the moon the old almanacs call the Beaver Moon, for the season when trappers set their lines before the ponds froze.||These last warm days are borrowed time, and the wise household spends them on the chores the cold will make miserable. With a freeze forecast for Wednesday morning, it is the weekend to drain and coil garden hoses, wrap the outdoor spigots, bring in the potted geraniums and ferns, and pick the last green tomatoes to ripen on a windowsill. Turnip and mustard greens and the collards will shrug off the frost and taste the sweeter for it.||In the yard and the woods, pecans are dropping under the old trees along the farm lanes, and the persimmons have turned soft and sugary on the bare limbs, a feast for possums, raccoons and the deer that slip out at dusk. The dry fall has left the ditches and sloughs low, so Sunday’s rain will be welcome on the land as well as at the duck holes.'))
body.append('<p><i>Weather lore, not a forecast:</i> an old saying holds, “When the wind is in the south, the rain is in its mouth,” for a steady south wind carries warm, moist Gulf air ahead of an approaching front. This weekend’s balmy southerly breezes and Sunday’s showers fit the rhyme nicely.</p>')
body.append('<h2>Of Local Interest</h2>')
body.append('<h3>Former deputy prosecutor sues Hagood, alleging she was fired over her pregnancy</h3>')
body.append(P('A former deputy prosecutor in the Second Judicial District, the prosecuting attorney’s office that handles criminal cases in Craighead, Mississippi and four other Northeast Arkansas counties, has sued her former boss in federal court, alleging she was fired days before her maternity leave was to begin. Presley Phillips Noel filed the suit Oct. 14 in the U.S. District Court for the Eastern District of Arkansas against Prosecuting Attorney Sonia Hagood and Chief Deputy Prosecutor Jessica Thomason; the case came to public notice this week.||According to the complaint, Ms. Noel joined the office in January 2023, had complications during her pregnancy, submitted her maternity leave paperwork on Jan. 8 of this year and was fired on Jan. 9, two months before her due date. She also says that a 2025 transfer to Mississippi County raised her workload from two dockets to six and added more than two hours of daily driving. As she describes the office’s answer to her federal discrimination complaint, it pointed to a poor May 2025 evaluation and claims of insubordination; she calls that evaluation false and notes she received a $32,000 merit raise two months after it.||The suit claims violations of the Family and Medical Leave Act, the Pregnancy Discrimination Act, the Pregnant Workers Fairness Act and the Constitution’s equal protection guarantee, and seeks more than $75,000 in damages, reinstatement or front pay, and a jury trial. These are allegations only; Ms. Hagood did not respond to a request for comment, and no answer had been filed in court as of Thursday. U.S. District Judge James M. Moody Jr. stepped aside from the case, which is being reassigned to another judge.'))
body.append('<h3>City Water &amp; Light trades wooden poles for steel</h3>')
body.append(P('City Water & Light, Jonesboro’s city-owned electric, water and sewer utility, is replacing wooden power poles across town with steel ones, and most are already in the ground. Slade Mitchell, the utility’s energy, marketing and purchasing director, said the poles were bought at “pennies on the dollar” from a federal surplus that had been intended for hurricane relief in Puerto Rico; no dollar figure was given.||The standard 70-foot steel poles are rated to withstand hurricanes and are expected to last about 50 years or more, against roughly 30 for wood, which the utility says should mean steadier power as winter comes on. Work remains in a few places, including a stretch of Matthews Avenue, and crews expect to finish over the coming months alongside other projects.'))
body.append('<h3>Fire marshal’s office offers Thanksgiving kitchen advice</h3>')
body.append(P('Thanksgiving is the worst day of the year for home cooking fires, and Jason Wills, division chief in the Jonesboro Fire Marshal’s Office, is asking families to take care. The National Fire Protection Association estimates fire departments answered 1,139 home cooking fires on Thanksgiving Day in 2024, more than three times the daily average.||Chief Wills advises staying in the kitchen while food cooks, keeping children back from the stove and never putting water on a grease fire; smother it with the pot’s lid instead, and keep that lid close at hand. Those frying a turkey should thaw it fully, not overfill the pot with oil and set up at least 10 feet from the house; grills belong outdoors, never in a garage or on a wooden deck. The Jonesboro Fire Department will install smoke detectors free of charge, and in a fire, get out and call 911.'))
body.append('<h3>At A-State, young entrepreneurs and a gift from a governor</h3>')
body.append(P('About 300 students in third through sixth grade from schools across the region came to the Reng Student Union at Arkansas State University on Thursday for the Small Business Leadership Conference held by A-State’s Scarlet and Black program. The young inventors pitched toys of their own design to judges, with commercials, print ads and elevator speeches; one Tyronza Elementary fourth-grader’s group offered a sensory fidget board called “Forever Fidgeting.” April Johnson, the gifted and talented facilitator for the Valley View School District, brought 15 fourth-graders and said the day builds teamwork, public speaking and creativity.||Across campus, former Gov. Mike Beebe, an A-State alumnus who led Arkansas from 2007 to 2015, has given the university’s Bradbury Art Museum a work called “Beau” by V. L. Cox, an Arkansas-raised artist now in New York. It comes from her series “Images of the American South,” built over three decades from vintage screen doors and other found objects. “This work of art is very special to me, and I want to share it with as many people as possible,” Mr. Beebe said; museum director Dr. Julia Wintner said it strengthens the museum’s collection of art about Arkansas and Southern history.'))
body.append('<h3>A Nettleton student’s wish comes true</h3>')
body.append(P('William King, a 13-year-old student at University Heights School of Medical Arts in the Nettleton School District, learned at a school celebration this week that the Make-A-Wish Foundation is sending him to Disney World. William has battled a genetic disorder since infancy and made his wish earlier this year. “I’m speechless right now. I don’t know what to say. I love it,” he said. A fan of all things mechanical, he said he most looks forward to building drones, and perhaps a lightsaber. He leaves Nov. 29.'))
body.append('<h3>Around town</h3>')
body.append(P('<b>Friday, Nov. 20.</b> Today is the 19th annual Fill the Food Bank drive for the Food Bank of Northeast Arkansas, 6 a.m. to 6 p.m., at 17 drop-off sites including the Jonesboro Walmart and Sam’s Club stores and a site in Brookland; the goal is 400,000 holiday meals. Gifts may also be made by texting NEAFOOD to 43414, at foodbankofnea.org or by calling (870) 932-3663. In Paragould, the high school presents the comedy “Leading Ladies” at 7 p.m. in the school auditorium; admission is pay what you can.||<b>Saturday, Nov. 21.</b> The Judd Hill Farmers Market on Aggie Road holds the second of its winter markets, 9 a.m. to 2 p.m., with a gingerbread competition and letters to Santa. “Leading Ladies” plays again at Paragould High School at 7 p.m.||<b>Sunday, Nov. 22.</b> NEA Baptist Memorial Hospital lights its Christmas tree at 5 p.m. in the lobby at 4800 E. Johnson Ave., with carols, Scripture, cocoa and cookies, and visits from Santa, Mrs. Claus and Elsa, who will read “The Night Before Christmas.” Free; socks and underwear for the Children’s Home of Paragould are welcome.||<b>Monday, Nov. 23.</b> KASU’s Bluegrass Monday brings the Nashville family band Williamson Branch to the Collins Theatre in Paragould for an all-holiday program at 7 p.m. (doors 6 p.m.; jam session 5 p.m. next door). Tickets $10, 18 and under free.||<b>Tuesday, Nov. 24.</b> The Jonesboro City Council’s Finance and Administration Committee meets at 4 p.m.; agendas are on the city website.||<b>Looking ahead.</b> The Junior Auxiliary of Jonesboro will give one free winter coat per child, with hot cocoa, at its Coats & Cocoa drive Dec. 9, 4 to 8 p.m., at the Parker Park Community Center gym, 1506 N. Church St. New or gently used coats of all sizes may be dropped off by Dec. 7 at Harris Furniture or NYC Sugaring.'))
body.append('<h3>Our Neighbors Farther Afield</h3>')
body.append(P('<b>Batesville.</b> The Independence County Sheriff’s Office is searching for Joshua Joel Adame, who ran from the county jail in Batesville about 4:05 p.m. Thursday wearing a white and green jail jumpsuit. He had been held on two misdemeanor failure-to-appear warrants, in theft and drug-paraphernalia cases, and the sheriff’s office says he is not believed to be armed or dangerous. Anyone who sees him should call 911.||<b>Newport.</b> The Jackson County Quorum Court, the county’s governing board of justices of the peace, voted Thursday to declare the county judge’s office vacant as of Jan. 1, following the retirement of County Judge Jeff Phillips after 16 years. Applicants for the appointment must send a résumé, cover letter and proof of qualifications to the county clerk’s office by 4:30 p.m. Dec. 14; the new court seated in January will make the choice within 30 days of Jan. 1, and the appointee may not run for the office at the next general election.'))
body.append('<div style="break-inside:avoid;page-break-inside:avoid"><h2>From the Police Reports</h2><p class="note">Jonesboro Police incident reports. Charges are allegations. No names are printed.</p><p>The latest batch covers 23 reports filed from Wednesday morning to early Thursday, with 9 arrests.</p></div>')
body.append('<p class="note" style="break-after:avoid;margin-bottom:2pt"><b>Overnight</b></p>')
body.append('<p><b>Corrections officer hurt; two arrested off Phillips Drive.</b> A suspicious-activity call near Phillips Drive just before 4 a.m. Thursday ended with a woman arrested on a felony charge of second-degree battery, accused of injuring a county corrections officer, plus criminal mischief for damage to a parked car and disorderly conduct. In a linked report, a man was arrested on charges of DWI, driving on a suspended license and an ignition-interlock violation.</p>')
body.append('<p><b>Two hit-and-runs in the morning commute.</b> Drivers left the scene of crashes Thursday at about 7 a.m. at Dean Street and East Johnson Avenue and about 7:35 a.m. at Clark Street and East Nettleton Avenue, each with an estimated $1,000 to $10,000 in damage. No one has been charged.</p>')
body.append('<p><b>Back door kicked in on Prospector Drive.</b> A home’s back door frame was broken Wednesday evening, a felony first-degree criminal mischief report with about $1,100 in damage.</p>')
body.append('<p><b>Threat at a behavioral health facility.</b> A man was arrested late Wednesday on a charge of second-degree terroristic threatening, accused of threatening to hit a staff member at a facility on East Johnson Avenue.</p>')
body.append('<p><b>Warrants and a traffic stop.</b> Two people were arrested overnight on warrants for unpaid city fines, one after a traffic stop on Red Wolf Boulevard and one near Olive Street and West Huntington Avenue, who was also cited for no proof of insurance. Separately, an officer on a welfare check off Prescott Lane was bitten by a dog; the bite left only a bruise.</p>')
body.append('<p><b>Also reported.</b> A third-degree domestic battery report, with one arrest, 72401 area.</p>')
body.append('<p class="note" style="break-after:avoid;margin-bottom:2pt"><b>Wednesday</b></p>')
body.append('<p><b>U-Haul moving truck never came back.</b> The U-Haul location on Red Wolf Boulevard reported a 26-foot moving truck, valued at about $30,000, rented Nov. 15 and not returned as agreed. It is being handled as a felony vehicle theft; no arrests yet.</p>')
body.append('<p><b>Washer and dryer taken in Bermuda Drive burglary.</b> People moving belongings out of a home on Bermuda Drive found a washer and dryer gone, a felony residential burglary report covering Monday evening to Wednesday morning.</p>')
body.append('<p><b>Fraud and a bad check.</b> A concrete contractor reported finishing a job off Sandstone Drive worth about $7,000 and being handed a $5,900 fictitious check (felony theft of services and second-degree forgery). A man on Southwest Drive reported identity fraud, with accounts opened in his name since late October, and a woman reported a phone and a wallet with her debit card stolen.</p>')
body.append('<p><b>Store thefts.</b> Ulta on East Highland Drive reported a felony theft of $1,000 to $5,000 involving two suspects; Rack Room Shoes on South Caraway Road reported a man who walked out in a new pair of shoes Monday; and a woman was cited for shoplifting at the East Johnson flea market, where she was also wanted on failure-to-appear and unpaid-fines warrants.</p>')
body.append('<p><b>Other items.</b> A woman was arrested on an unpaid-fines warrant after a trespass complaint at a gas station on East Highland Drive, and a small bag of suspected methamphetamine was found on the ground by a gas pump on Harrisburg Road. Also: an assault and disorderly-conduct report, juvenile suspect, 72401 area; and an interference-with-visitation report, 72401 area.</p>')
body.append('<p><i>A word to the wise:</i> If someone pays for work or goods with a check you don’t know, call the issuing bank to confirm it before you start or hand anything over, and if your wallet or phone goes missing, call your bank right away to lock your cards.</p>')
body.append('<div><p class="note" style="break-after:avoid">At the county jails</p><p>The Craighead County jail roster listed 312 people held Friday morning, with 29 booked in the last 48 hours; among those listed first, the most common entries were holds for other agencies, including several for out-of-state authorities, and domestic assault charges, along with a drug possession count and a felony probation violation, and no bond amounts were shown. In Randolph County, the sheriff’s roster listed 62 people held, with three booked since Wednesday evening on a release-conditions violation, a failure to appear and drug possession charges. Five people were released there in the last 48 hours, and no notable bonds were listed for the newest bookings. Charges are allegations, and being on a roster does not mean anyone has been convicted.</p></div>')
body.append('<h2>From the State</h2>')
body.append('<h3>Lawsuit takes aim at Arkansas’ ban on buying soda and candy with SNAP</h3>')
body.append(P('A federal class-action lawsuit filed Wednesday seeks to overturn the U.S. Department of Agriculture waivers that let Arkansas and 17 other states bar food-stamp shoppers from buying soda, candy and similar items. SNAP, the Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program, is the federal grocery benefit for low-income households. Arkansas’ waiver, sought by Gov. Sarah Huckabee Sanders and approved by the USDA, took effect July 1 and forbids SNAP purchases of soda (diet and zero-calorie included), candy, energy drinks and certain prepared desserts. || The suit was brought by the National Center for Law and Economic Justice, a New York-based advocacy group for low-income people. It argues that the USDA overstepped its legal authority by letting states redefine what counts as eligible food, skipped required procedures before granting the waivers, and left both shoppers and grocers confused by a patchwork of state rules. It also says some recipients rely on the banned items for chronic health conditions, and that stores risk penalties for an honest mistake at the register. “A backdoor method to restrict access to SNAP,” the center’s Katharine Deabler-Meadows called the waivers. || The governor’s office says the plan returns SNAP to its purpose of nutrition and fits the federal “Make America Healthy Again” campaign; the USDA has defended the waivers as giving states room to encourage healthier choices. The filing changes nothing for now: Arkansas’ restrictions stay in force unless a court orders otherwise. Shoppers unsure of an item can check it with the state Department of Human Services’ SNAP Companion App, which scans barcodes.'))
body.append('<h3>Exceptional drought now covers more than half of Arkansas</h3>')
body.append(P('More than 55 percent of Arkansas is now in “exceptional” drought, the most severe category on the U.S. Drought Monitor, the weekly federal map of dry conditions, according to Thursday’s update. Every part of the state is in some stage of drought heading into Thanksgiving week, and a dozen or more counties in north-central Arkansas remain in moderate to severe drought despite scattered showers. || Jeff Hood, a National Weather Service meteorologist in Little Rock, said this summer was among the hottest on record and that the state saw some of its five driest months on record; in places the rainfall shortfall stretches back more than a year. Lakes across the state sit below their normal levels, and Lake Maumelle, the main water supply for Little Rock and North Little Rock, is expected to fall 13.5 feet below full by the end of December without a good soaking. || County judges have ordered burn bans across much of south and Central Arkansas, including Pulaski County, and wildfire danger is rated high there. With most crops harvested, the state Department of Agriculture reminds farmers that crop-residue burning is allowed only when field edges have been properly disked. The outlook offers little help: highs in the 70s this weekend, a cold front next week with several freezing nights, and little or no rain through much of the week.'))
body.append('<h3>Report: Most new mothers who lose Medicaid at 60 days have no documented insurance</h3>')
body.append(P('A new state report, set for review this morning by the Arkansas Legislative Council (the panel of lawmakers that oversees state government between sessions), shows how many new mothers lose health coverage two months after giving birth. Arkansas pays for pregnancy care through Medicaid, the joint federal-state health program for low-income people, but ends that coverage 60 days after delivery. It is the only state that has not lengthened the period to 12 months. || According to the Department of Human Services report, 5,039 women reached the end of postpartum coverage between July 1 and Sept. 30. About 62 percent stayed eligible for some other form of Medicaid, but 1,902 lost coverage, and only 129 of those, roughly 7 percent, were recorded as having other insurance afterward. The department says that figure undercounts, since it reflects only women who reported new coverage on their own. Thirty-one of the mothers who lost coverage were teenagers. || Christin Harper of Arkansas Advocates for Children and Families, a nonprofit policy group, called for “extending pregnancy Medicaid to 12 months.” Gov. Sanders has opposed the change as “duplicative” of other coverage; her office pointed to the Healthy Moms, Healthy Babies Act, which directs $45 million a year to maternal and infant health. The issue is likely to return when the Legislature convenes its regular session Jan. 11.'))
body.append('<h3>Griffin announces six arrests in year-long massage-parlor trafficking probe</h3>')
body.append(P('Arkansas Attorney General Tim Griffin’s office announced Thursday that a year-long investigation into illicit massage businesses in Northwest Arkansas has produced six arrests, an effort his office described as the largest criminal operation it has ever carried out. Investigators believe the businesses were part of networks that lured women from China with promises of jobs in the United States, then forced them into commercial sex work. || The inquiry began in October 2025. On Oct. 6 of this year, the attorney general’s Special Investigations Division and 16 other federal, state and local agencies served search warrants at four businesses in Rogers, Springdale and Fayetteville and at a Springdale home, making five arrests. The sixth suspect was arrested Tuesday in California by the U.S. Marshals Service. || All six are charged with engaging in a continuing criminal gang, organization or enterprise; laundering criminal proceeds; and second-degree promoting prostitution. The charges are allegations, and none has been convicted. Another suspect remains at large and is believed to have fled to China by way of Mexico. Three women were offered help during the October raids, and two accepted services; the investigation continues.'))
body.append('<h3>Walmart raises its outlook, but shares slip</h3>')
body.append(P('Walmart, the Bentonville-based retailer that is Arkansas’ largest company, reported Thursday that sales rose 5.2 percent to $188.8 billion in the quarter ended Oct. 31, while net income fell 19.5 percent to about $4.9 billion. Adjusted earnings of 65 cents a share edged past Wall Street’s 64-cent forecast. Online sales grew 17 percent worldwide, and its U.S. stores saw sales at established locations climb 2.9 percent; higher diesel prices added roughly $2 billion in costs. || Chief Executive John Furner said the company was “growing our core business and gaining market share.” Walmart raised its full-year forecast, now expecting sales growth of 4.9 to 5.2 percent and earnings of $2.84 to $2.89 a share. Investors were unmoved: the stock fell more than 2 percent Thursday to $124.67, though it remains up nearly 24 percent this year.'))
body.append('<h3>In brief</h3>')
body.append(P('<b>Little Rock schools hold off on own police force.</b> The Little Rock School Board has unanimously tabled a proposal to replace its Little Rock Police Department officers with a district-run force of 12 certified officers at the high schools and armed security officers elsewhere. Members seemed to favor the idea but wanted more community input; no new date was set. || <b>Fires puzzle a Pulaski County community.</b> About a dozen vacant homes have burned over three months in Sweet Home, a rural, largely Black community in Pulaski County, three of them since last Saturday. The county sheriff’s office says it is investigating; residents suspect arson, but no cause or suspect has been named. || <b>Google pledges $10 million for Arkansas water.</b> The technology company will spend $10 million on water projects across the state, beginning with a $750,000 grant to the conservation group Ducks Unlimited for trails, a pavilion and outdoor classrooms at Ducks Unlimited Park in the lower Mississippi River floodplain. || <b>Jacksonville switches to ward voting.</b> Jacksonville voters narrowly approved, 3,063 to 3,024, a petition measure pushed by the local NAACP chapter to elect City Council members by ward rather than citywide. The change applies to future council elections, not the Dec. 1 runoffs; results are unofficial until certified.'))
body.append('<h2>From the Nation</h2>')
body.append('<h3>Anthropic’s record stock debut lands on a hard day for markets</h3>')
body.append(P('Anthropic, the San Francisco artificial-intelligence company that makes the Claude family of AI models, began trading on the Nasdaq stock exchange on Thursday under the ticker ANT. It is the largest initial public offering (a company’s first sale of shares to the public) on record. The shares were priced at $83 the night before, above the $70-to-$78 range the company had marketed, and the offering was enlarged to 980 million shares because of demand. Underwriters said the shares were as much as seven times oversubscribed, and about a fifth were set aside for ordinary investors. || The price valued Anthropic at about $1.5 trillion. Fortune put the money raised at about $79 billion, and more if underwriters use an option to sell extra shares, which tops the roughly $75 billion SpaceX raised in its June debut. The stock opened at $111.50, up 34 percent, then faded through the afternoon to close at $97.17, still 17 percent above the offering price. It did so on a bad day for the wider market: the Dow Jones Industrial Average fell about 1,100 points, and all three major indexes closed more than 2 percent lower, with technology shares weighed down by worries over Nvidia’s sales to China. || The company’s filings show revenue of $33.2 billion for the nine months through September, up from $2.5 billion a year earlier. Fortune reported a net loss of about $104 billion over the same period, mostly from the way the company accounted for convertible notes. The prospectus also warned that, without safeguards, the technology could pose “catastrophic or existential risks to humanity.” Anthropic is organized as a public benefit corporation, a type of company that must weigh a stated public mission alongside profit, and its chief executive, Dario Amodei, and six other co-founders keep majority voting control through special classes of stock.'))
body.append('<h3>White House cyber chief out after a week of outages</h3>')
body.append(P('Sean Cairncross, the national cyber director, is leaving the Trump administration, the White House confirmed Thursday. His office coordinates cybersecurity policy across the federal government, and he had led it since August 2025. His exit follows a week of technology failures, including last Friday’s nationwide halt to commercial flights, an outage of a key system at the National Security Agency, slowed bank transactions and email disruptions. || Accounts of why he is going differ. A source told CNN that President Trump “fully supported firing” Mr. Cairncross because he “lacked the strong cyber credentials to perform the job,” while Fox News reported that he did not wish to serve two more years, and Axios described the parting as by mutual agreement. Kirsten Davies, the Defense Department’s chief information officer, will serve as acting director; a permanent pick needs Senate confirmation. || Administration officials and Democrats in Congress have said the outages do not appear to be cyberattacks, but no full public explanation has been given for several of them. Mr. Trump has called them unconnected “individual events.”'))
body.append('<h3>Senate passes bill to speed permits for energy projects</h3>')
body.append(P('The Senate voted 62 to 33 on Thursday to pass the Bipartisan American Affordability and Jobs Act, a 500-page measure meant to shorten the federal permitting process that can delay power plants, transmission lines, mines and data centers for years. The bill was a compromise worked out with the White House after years of on-and-off talks. Fifteen Democrats joined nearly all Republicans in support; 31 Democrats and two Republicans, Sens. Mike Rounds of South Dakota and Josh Hawley of Missouri, voted no. || Supporters, including Republican Sens. Shelley Moore Capito of West Virginia and John Barrasso of Wyoming, say it will make it easier to build both renewable and fossil-fuel projects and new high-voltage lines by streamlining environmental and court reviews and limiting lawsuits. Opponents, including Democratic Sens. Jeff Merkley and Ron Wyden of Oregon and groups such as the Sierra Club, say it would weaken environmental protections, favor fossil fuels and ease approval of power-hungry data centers. One provision would require large new data centers to pay for the grid upgrades built to serve them. || The bill now goes to the House, where its fate is uncertain. A spokesman for House Democratic leader Hakeem Jeffries said it “falls short in several areas,” and some House Republicans want changes to the transmission sections. President Trump has said he would sign it.'))
body.append('<h3>Army delays blast of unstable explosives in Iowa</h3>')
body.append(P('The Army has put off until Monday its plan to blow up about five tons of degraded lead azide, a highly sensitive explosive used to set off larger charges, at the Iowa Army Ammunition Plant in Middletown, a town of about 360 people in southeast Iowa near the Mississippi River. The detonation had been set for noon Friday. Acting Army Secretary Adam Telle announced the delay Thursday evening, saying “public safety is the Army’s top priority,” and that Monday is the latest safe moment before a forecast cold snap, since cold raises the risk the material could explode on its own. || The 68 barrels were stored for more than 20 years, and an inspection this year found that water keeping the explosive stable had partly evaporated. The Army says moving them is too dangerous. Officials expect the blast to send a plume of “some lead-containing particles” downwind, possibly into northwest Illinois; lead is a toxin that is especially harmful to young children. || Lawmakers of both parties in Iowa and Illinois criticized the short notice, since many communities as far as 100 miles away learned of the risk only on Sunday, and Gov. Kim Reynolds asked for the delay. Environmental groups led by the Sierra Club’s Iowa chapter have asked a federal appeals court to block the Environmental Protection Agency’s emergency permit. Residents are advised to stay indoors with windows shut, bring in animals and feed, and wet-clean outdoor surfaces afterward.'))
body.append('<h3>California subpoenas Nvidia over former chief’s ties to a suspected spy</h3>')
body.append(P('California Attorney General Rob Bonta issued an investigative subpoena to Nvidia, the Santa Clara company whose chips power most artificial-intelligence systems, his office said Thursday. He is examining whether actions by Nvidia or its former chief executive, Jensen Huang, let China obtain advanced chips or confidential information in violation of U.S. export limits, and whether the company misled investors about that risk. “Companies cannot mislead their investors,” Mr. Bonta said. || Mr. Huang resigned last week after admitting to extramarital affairs, one reportedly with Alice Huang (no relation), a former executive of Megaspeed, a Singapore company under investigation for allegedly diverting restricted Nvidia chips to China. Reports have described her as a suspected Chinese spy, but U.S. Attorney General Todd Blanche has said neither the Justice Department nor the FBI has confirmed that, and no public evidence shows Nvidia technology was improperly passed on. Nvidia has opened its own inquiry and says it has “no reason to believe” its secrets or national security were compromised. Its shares have fallen about 22 percent since Mr. Huang admitted the affair.'))
body.append('<h3>Record flooding in Florida as a storm eyes the East Coast</h3>')
body.append(P('The St. Johns River near Cocoa, on Florida’s Space Coast, rose above its all-time record on Thursday, passing a mark set in October 1953, after another 6 to 8 inches of rain fell overnight on already soaked Brevard County. Rescue crews, including the Florida National Guard, pulled people from flooded homes in Cocoa, Palm Bay residents near a canal were ordered to evacuate, and Brevard schools are closed until after Thanksgiving. The river was expected to crest around 18.2 feet on Friday, with a flood watch into Saturday evening. || The low-pressure system behind the rain is expected to move north off the Southeast coast this weekend. The National Hurricane Center gives it a medium chance of briefly becoming a tropical or subtropical storm, which would be named Kyle. Either way, forecasters expect strong winds, dangerous surf and coastal flooding from Florida to the mid-Atlantic into early next week, just as early Thanksgiving travel begins.'))
body.append('<h3>In brief</h3>')
body.append(P('<b>Bank vendor breach.</b> JPMorgan Chase confirmed Thursday that hackers broke into outside vendors holding some of its marketing data, which may include customer names and email addresses; Visa and Mastercard disclosed similar supplier incidents. A group calling itself n0n has posted stolen files online, which researchers say include roughly 16,000 email addresses, though the data has not been independently verified. Chase says no passwords, card numbers or balances were taken, its own systems were not breached, and the incident is unrelated to its outage earlier in the week. || <b>Trump to meet the press.</b> President Trump, back in Washington from his Asia trip, said he would hold a White House news conference at noon Eastern (11 a.m. Central) Friday to discuss the trip and new trade agreements with Thailand, the Philippines and other countries.'))
body.append('<h2>From Abroad</h2>')
body.append('<h3>Russia pounds Ukraine; a Russian missile comes down in Poland</h3>')
body.append(P('Russia launched one of the largest air attacks of the war overnight into Friday, firing more than 110 missiles and over 400 attack drones at Ukraine, according to Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy. In Kyiv, local authorities said at least three people were killed and nine injured; a ballistic missile damaged the Podilskyi Bridge over the Dnipro River, and the Darnytskyi and Southern bridges were also hit, and parts of five city districts lost power. The strikes came hours after President Vladimir Putin, speaking in China, vowed to answer three Ukrainian attacks this week on the Kerch Bridge, the road-and-rail link between Russia and occupied Crimea. || During the barrage, a Russian Kh-101 cruise missile crashed in a field near the village of Nowy Lipowiec in eastern Poland, a NATO member, Polish Defense Minister Władysław Kosiniak-Kamysz said. Polish authorities are investigating two other sites in the southeast, which they believe were a second missile and a jet-powered drone. No one was hurt and no important infrastructure was damaged. Mr. Kosiniak-Kamysz called it “yet another provocation by Russia,” noting that the countries backing Ukraine, known as the Coalition of the Willing, were holding exercises nearby that day, though Warsaw has not said Poland was the intended target. Prime Minister Donald Tusk called in ministers, military and intelligence chiefs, and the Russian embassy did not respond to requests for comment. || The attack followed a Thursday meeting in Berlin where Mr. Zelenskyy, leaders of more than 40 partner countries and President Trump’s envoys Steve Witkoff and Jared Kushner discussed peace efforts. Mr. Trump phoned in from Air Force One and, by several accounts, told skeptical Europeans that Mr. Putin wants to end the war.'))
body.append('<h3>Shenzhen “peace declaration” draws doubts at home and abroad</h3>')
body.append(P('The joint statement that came out of Thursday’s first-ever meeting of President Trump, China’s leader Xi Jinping and Russia’s Vladimir Putin, held in Shenzhen during the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation summit, turned out to be four short pledges. The three agreed that nuclear war cannot be won and that nuclear risks should be reduced, that oil supplies should increase, that the United Nations needs unspecified reforms, and that state officials should be shielded from what they called “jurisdiction abuse” by the International Criminal Court, the war-crimes court in The Hague that has an arrest warrant out for Mr. Putin. || The text says nothing about Russia’s war in Ukraine, the U.S. war with Iran or Taiwan, and sets no date for nuclear arms talks. Mr. Trump rated the meeting “12 out of 10,” and Mr. Xi called it “positive and fruitful.” Senate Democratic leader Chuck Schumer called the trip a “historical embarrassment,” and CBS News and the Associated Press described the outcome as more symbol than substance. || Mr. Trump also left China without the meeting with North Korea’s Kim Jong Un he had floated, blaming “too many meetings” and saying “we’ll have that meeting at some point.” The same day, Rafael Grossi, head of the International Atomic Energy Agency, the U.N. nuclear watchdog, told its board that North Korea has finished the outside of a new extension to its uranium-enrichment plant at Yongbyon, its main nuclear site, calling the expansion “extremely worrying.”'))
body.append('<h3>Israeli tanks cross the Gaza truce line in a rare raid</h3>')
body.append(P('Israeli tanks pushed west of the “Yellow Line,” the boundary that has divided Israeli-held and Palestinian-held Gaza since the U.S.-brokered ceasefire of October 2025, in a raid into the Nuseirat refugee camp in central Gaza that began Wednesday. The military said it was destroying a Hamas tunnel that threatened its troops and announced Thursday that the operation had ended and the main north-south road had reopened. It was the first time since the truce that Israel publicly announced a raid that deep past the line. || Gaza’s Hamas-run government media office said about 6,000 people fled the area; some returned Thursday while others stayed away, citing gunfire. Hamas accused Israel of trying to wreck the ceasefire, and Defense Minister Israel Katz said the army is ready to take more land. Gaza health officials say at least 1,600 Palestinians have been killed since the truce began, and the Israeli military says five of its soldiers have been killed in that time.'))
body.append('<h3>Climate talks near their end with fossil-fuel language dropped</h3>')
body.append(P('The United Nations climate summit known as COP31, in Antalya, Turkey, reached its scheduled final day on Friday with a slimmed-down draft that leaves out any explicit mention of moving away from fossil fuels. The new nine-page text, half the length of the earlier draft, also drops a proposed target of $120 billion a year by 2035 to help poorer countries adapt to climate change, though it keeps an earlier pledge to triple such aid. It retains a goal of raising electricity’s share of world energy use from about 21 percent to 35 percent by 2035. || The summit’s Turkish president, Murat Kurum, called the text the “Antalya Declaration,” and Australia’s Chris Bowen, who is leading the negotiations, called it the best basis for agreement. Campaigners called it a “backward step,” and Panama has vowed to block any outcome that does not meet its demands, including a cap on oil production. U.N. talks often run past their deadline, and this one may stretch into the weekend. The United States did not take part directly for the second year in a row.'))
body.append('<h3>Somalia expels the United Arab Emirates’ ambassador</h3>')
body.append(P('Somalia’s government on Thursday gave the ambassador of the United Arab Emirates, the Gulf oil state, 72 hours to leave and recalled its own envoy from Abu Dhabi. Information Minister Abdifatah Kasim Mohamud accused the Emirates of violating Somalia’s sovereignty in what he called an “open invasion,” without describing a specific new act. || Somali President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud said this week that the Emirates had signed port deals at Berbera, Bosaso and Kismayo directly with regional governments that oppose the national government in Mogadishu, and had helped set up forces outside the national army’s command. Somalia canceled all its agreements with the Emirates in January. The Emirati foreign ministry condemned the expulsion as “escalatory and hostile” and said it would harm relations.'))
body.append('<h3>In brief</h3>')
body.append(P('<b>Mongolia’s president steps down.</b> Mongolia’s parliament voted Thursday, 96 of its 104 members in favor, to accept the resignation of President Ukhnaa Khurelsukh, who quit after a rival in his own ruling party accused him of taking bribes tied to a railway project years ago, when he was prime minister. Mr. Khurelsukh denies wrongdoing; the parliament speaker serves as acting president, and a new election must be held within four months. || <b>South Korea ruling.</b> South Korea’s Supreme Court on Thursday threw out former Prime Minister Han Duck-soo’s conviction for aiding ousted President Yoon Suk Yeol’s brief 2024 martial-law decree, ruling 9 to 3 that prosecutors had not proved his intent, and sent the case back to the Seoul High Court, which had sentenced him to 15 years. He remains in detention, and the ruling Democratic Party has threatened to seek the chief justice’s impeachment. The court upheld a four-year prison term for Mr. Yoon’s wife, Kim Keon Hee, on corruption charges. || <b>Bukele seeks a third term.</b> El Salvador’s President Nayib Bukele filed Thursday night to run for a third straight term in the February election, made possible by a 2025 constitutional change that ended presidential term limits. Polls put his approval near 90 percent because of his crackdown on gangs, while rights groups and opponents call the bid illegal and accuse him of concentrating power.'))
body.append('<div style="break-inside:avoid;page-break-inside:avoid"><h2>Markets &amp; the Farm</h2><p class="note">Figures are as of Thursday’s close, November 19. Week changes are measured from the close of Friday, November 13.</p><table class="mk"><tr><th>Index</th><th>Close</th><th>Day</th><th>Week</th></tr><tr><td>S&amp;P 500</td><td class="n">7,175.83</td><td class="n">−147.80 (−2.0%)</td><td class="n">−385.84 (−5.1%)</td></tr><tr><td>Dow Jones Industrials</td><td class="n">49,097.06</td><td class="n">−1,101.11 (−2.2%)</td><td class="n">−3,250.49 (−6.2%)</td></tr><tr><td>Nasdaq Composite</td><td class="n">23,879.20</td><td class="n">−550.69 (−2.3%)</td><td class="n">−1,257.07 (−5.0%)</td></tr></table><p>Wall Street had a hard Thursday. Stocks gave up an early rally and slid through the afternoon; the Dow lost more than 1,100 points, every one of the 11 market sectors finished lower, and the S&amp;P 500 fell for a sixth straight day, its longest losing streak since April 2024. Worries about lofty prices for artificial-intelligence stocks and the chance of further Federal Reserve rate increases outweighed the stock-market debut of Anthropic, the AI company, whose shares closed up about 17 percent on their first day of trading.</p><p>Next week is a short one because of Thanksgiving, and traders will be watching whether the selling eases before the holiday.</p></div>')
body.append('<h3>What analysts are saying</h3>')
body.append('<ul class="picks"><li><b>Analysts leaned toward buying: ABT</b> (Abbott Laboratories, maker of medical devices, diagnostic tests and nutrition products such as Similac and Ensure). Leerink Partners raised it to outperform from market perform on Thursday and lifted its price target to $124 from $106, saying the balance of risk and reward now “leans squarely positive,” helped by growth in medical devices. Risk: the target assumes steady earnings growth through 2027, which slower diagnostics or nutrition sales could undercut.</li><li><b>Analysts leaned toward buying: MDB</b> (MongoDB, which sells database software that developers use to build apps, largely through its Atlas cloud service). UBS upgraded it to buy from neutral on Thursday with a $450 target, up from $375, saying checks with more than 10 customers and partners point to steady demand and a meaningful share of new AI workloads. Risk: the company reports results Dec. 1, and a miss on cloud growth could quickly undo the gain.</li><li><b>Analysts leaned toward buying: BOX</b> (Box, which provides cloud file storage and document-management software for businesses). UBS also upgraded Box to buy from neutral, raising its target to $55 from $37, on faster growth as companies pay for higher-tier plans to run AI tools on their stored documents. Risk: the higher target leans on a richer valuation, and the shares could fall back if those upgrades come more slowly.</li><li><b>Analysts turned cautious: AON</b> (Aon, a global insurance broker and risk consultant). Wells Fargo cut it to equal weight from overweight on Thursday, keeping a $364 target, citing the risks of absorbing its $17 billion purchase of USI Insurance Services and a pause in share buybacks. Risk to the cautious view: if the deal’s savings arrive on schedule, the shares could recover faster than expected.</li><li><b>Analysts turned cautious: AJG</b> (Arthur J. Gallagher, an insurance brokerage). In the same Wells Fargo note, Gallagher was cut to equal weight with a $294 target, down from $312, on expectations that its organic growth will slow to 4 percent in 2027 from 4.7 percent this year, while the shares trade at a premium to rivals. Risk to the cautious view: firmer insurance pricing could keep growth stronger than forecast. <i>(Aon and Gallagher calls: single source, one Wells Fargo note.)</i></li></ul>')
body.append('<p class="note">This is information about what analysts said, not advice to buy or sell.</p>')
body.append('<h3>Commodities</h3>')
body.append('<table class="mk split"><tr><th>Commodity</th><th>Close</th><th>Change</th></tr><tr><td>WTI crude (Dec.), per bbl.</td><td class="n">$83.21</td><td class="n">−$0.05</td></tr><tr><td>Brent crude (Jan.), per bbl.</td><td class="n">$91.98</td><td class="n">+$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>Arkansas avg. regular gas (AAA, Nov. 19)</td><td class="n">$3.37</td><td class="n">−18¢ on week</td></tr><tr><td>Jonesboro metro avg. gas (AAA, Nov. 19)</td><td class="n">$3.35</td><td class="n">−13¢ on week</td></tr><tr><td>Live cattle (Dec.), per cwt</td><td class="n">$222.85</td><td class="n">+$3.07</td></tr><tr><td>Feeder cattle (Jan.), per cwt</td><td class="n">$335.55</td><td class="n">+$4.55</td></tr><tr><td>Lean hogs (Dec.), per cwt</td><td class="n">$52.42</td><td class="n">+$1.07</td></tr><tr><td>Corn (Dec.), per bu.</td><td class="n">$4.39</td><td class="n">+3½¢</td></tr><tr><td>Soybeans (Jan.), per bu.</td><td class="n">$12.07½</td><td class="n">−9¼¢</td></tr><tr><td>Wheat, Chicago (Dec.), per bu.</td><td class="n">$5.78¼</td><td class="n">+8¼¢</td></tr><tr><td>Rough rice (Jan.), per cwt</td><td class="n">$18.65½</td><td class="n">+17¢</td></tr><tr><td>Cotton (Dec.), per lb.</td><td class="n">75.98¢</td><td class="n">+0.98¢</td></tr></table>')
body.append('<p class="note">Oil ended nearly flat as a bigger-than-expected rise in U.S. crude and gasoline stockpiles canceled early gains on Middle East shipping worries; one farm wire showed December WTI at $83.98, up 72 cents, and the exchange settlement reported by DTN is used here. Cattle led a broad farm rally while soybeans slipped. Gas in Arkansas fell 18 cents in a week ahead of Thanksgiving travel. Grains, livestock, rice and cotton: single source (Brownfield).</p>')
body.append('<h2>On the Field</h2>')
body.append(P('<b>Arkansas State football</b> (4-6, 2-4 Sun Belt) plays its first game since the firing of head coach Butch Jones on Saturday at Louisiana Tech, 6 p.m. Central at Joe Aillet Stadium in Ruston, La., streaming on ESPN+. Jalen Ortiz, the associate head coach and cornerbacks coach who joined the staff in 2025, leads the team as interim coach for the rest of the season. The task is steep: Louisiana Tech (8-2, 6-0) has won seven straight and already clinched the Sun Belt West, and the Red Wolves must win both remaining games, Saturday’s and the home finale against Troy, to reach the six wins needed for a bowl.||The search for a permanent coach goes on, with the search firm TurnkeyZRG helping. The RedWolfReport website, citing unnamed sources, reported that Hugh Freeze, who led A-State to a 10-2 season in 2011 before coaching at Ole Miss and Auburn, is at the top of the university’s list; that report has not been confirmed by the school, and no hire had been announced as of Friday morning.'))
body.append(P('<b>Arkansas State men’s basketball</b> (3-2) heads to Miami for a three-game event hosted by Florida International after Wednesday’s 100-69 rout of Arkansas-Pine Bluff, in which Jordan Pyke scored 20 points and Delang Muon had 15 points and 10 rebounds, his first career double-double. The Red Wolves open against Southern Utah on Monday, Nov. 23, at 2:30 p.m. Central, then play Long Beach State on Tuesday and FIU on Wednesday, all on the EAB Red Wolves Sports Network radio broadcasts. They are home again against Rice on Sunday, Nov. 29.'))
body.append(P('<b>Arkansas Razorbacks football</b> (4-6, 2-5 SEC) visits No. 5 Texas (8-2, 5-2) on Saturday at 2:30 p.m. Central in Austin, listed on ESPN. The Hogs, in their first season under coach Ryan Silverfield, come in buoyed by last week’s 34-6 home rout of a ranked South Carolina team, but Texas, led by quarterback Arch Manning, has won four straight and is a heavy favorite. The Longhorns lead the old Southwest Conference rivalry 58-23, and Arkansas has not won in Austin since 2003.'))
body.append(P('<b>Tennessee Vols football</b> (8-2, 5-2 SEC), No. 9 in the playoff rankings, closes its home schedule against LSU (6-4, 3-4) on Senior Day Saturday at 2:30 p.m. Central at Neyland Stadium in Knoxville, on ABC. LSU is coached by Lane Kiffin, who coached the Vols for one season in 2009. The question is quarterback George MacIntyre, who hurt his right knee in last week’s win over Texas A&amp;M; coach Josh Heupel said Monday he “looked good,” and MacIntyre was not on the SEC’s early availability report, though the final one comes before kickoff. Receiver Mike Matthews is out for the season, one of 10 Vols lost for the year.'))
body.append('<h2>Afield &amp; Afloat</h2>')
body.append('<p class="note">Ratings are rule-of-thumb estimates from the forecast, the rut calendar and the sun, not measurements of animal or fish behavior.</p>')
body.append('<h3>Deer outlook: Good</h3>')
body.append(P('The rut is still inside its peak window for north Arkansas, the wind is nearly calm at dawn, about 4 to 5 mph, and the barometer, high near 30.29 inches at first light, eases down through the day, which often gets deer on their feet ahead of a change. The one drawback is heat: a 46-degree dawn becomes a 73-degree afternoon, and mature deer bed early on warm days. Best windows: from 6:14 a.m. to about two hours after sunrise, and the last two hours of light, 2:52 to 4:52 p.m.; with the rut on, 10 a.m. to 2 p.m. is also realistic for a buck cruising between doe groups.||<i>Today’s tip:</i> on a warm, light-wind day, scent drifts and pools rather than blowing away, so set up where the thermals carry it off your trail: low in the bottoms early, when cool air slides downhill, and on the ridges in the afternoon, when the warming air rises.'))
body.append(P('<b>Tomorrow, Saturday (estimate: Good).</b> Much the same: a dawn in the upper 40s, an afternoon near 73 and light wind around 5 to 7 mph, with the barometer falling another notch toward Sunday’s front. Windows from 6:15 a.m. to about two hours after sunrise, 10 a.m. to 2 p.m. for cruising bucks, and 2:51 to 4:51 p.m. <i>Tomorrow’s tip:</i> Saturday and Sunday are the last two days of modern gun in Zone 4, and Sunday looks wet, so make Saturday count; sit a pinch point near water, since deer moving in the heat will drink at midday, and the drought has narrowed where they can.'))
body.append('<h3>Fishing outlook: Good</h3>')
body.append(P('A warm, sunny afternoon near 73, a light chop and a slow fall in the barometer make for a good day on Craighead Forest Lake and Lake Frierson. Best times: late morning through mid-afternoon as the sun warms the shallows, and the last hour before the 4:52 sunset.||<i>Today’s tip:</i> fall crappie gather on brush piles and around the fishing piers in 8 to 12 feet of water; a small minnow under a slip bobber, set just above the brush, keeps the bait in the strike zone without hanging up. Bass will chase shad toward the backs of coves in the warm afternoon, so a shad-colored crankbait or spinnerbait is worth a few casts there.'))
body.append(P('<b>Tomorrow, Saturday (estimate: Good).</b> Another warm afternoon near 74 with a light breeze, and the pressure dropping ahead of Sunday’s front, which often sets fish feeding. Best times: late morning to mid-afternoon and the last hour before the 4:51 sunset. <i>Tomorrow’s tip:</i> the day before a front is a day to cover water; try a jig or a soft-plastic craw along the riprap and points for bass, and get your fishing in before Sunday’s rain and the colder week that follows chill the shallows.'))
body.append('<h3>In season now, and what is next (zones 3, 4, 6 and 8)</h3>')
body.append('<table class="mk"><tr><th>Zone</th><th>Archery</th><th>Youth gun</th><th>Modern gun</th><th>Muzzleloader</th></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Sept. 26, 2026 – Feb. 28, 2027</td><td>Nov. 7-8, 2026; Jan. 2-3, 2027</td><td>Nov. 14 – Dec. 6; Dec. 26-28, 2026</td><td>Oct. 17-25; Dec. 12-14, 2026</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Sept. 26, 2026 – Feb. 28, 2027</td><td>Nov. 7-8, 2026; Jan. 2-3, 2027</td><td>Nov. 14-22; Dec. 26-28, 2026</td><td>Closed</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Sept. 26, 2026 – Feb. 28, 2027</td><td>Nov. 7-8, 2026; Jan. 2-3, 2027</td><td>Nov. 14 – Dec. 6; Dec. 26-28, 2026</td><td>Oct. 17-25; Dec. 12-14, 2026</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Sept. 26, 2026 – Feb. 28, 2027</td><td>Nov. 7-8, 2026; Jan. 2-3, 2027</td><td>Nov. 14 – Dec. 6; Dec. 26-28, 2026</td><td>Oct. 17-25; Dec. 12-14, 2026</td></tr></table>')
body.append(P('<b>Deer, this weekend and next.</b> This is the last weekend of modern gun season in Zone 4, which takes in Craighead County: it closes after Sunday, Nov. 22, and reopens only for the Dec. 26-28 holiday hunt, while archery stays open through Feb. 28. Zones 3, 6 and 8 remain open to modern gun through Dec. 6, and those zones get a second muzzleloader weekend Dec. 12-14; Zone 4 has no muzzleloader season. The bag limit is four deer, no more than two bucks; Zone 4 allows only one antlerless deer by modern gun. Outside the CWD Management Zone, bucks in Zones 3, 4 and 8 must have at least three points on one side. Dogs are not allowed for deer in Zones 3 and 4; in Zones 6 and 8 they are allowed through Dec. 6.'))
body.append(P('<b>Waterfowl.</b> Duck season opens Saturday, Nov. 21, 30 minutes before sunrise, and runs in three segments: Nov. 21-29, Dec. 10-23 and Dec. 26 to Jan. 31. The daily limit is six ducks, with no more than four mallards (two hens), three wood ducks, three pintails and one scaup; most wildlife management areas close to hunting at noon. Canada, white-fronted and light geese are open in the same segments, with daily limits of two Canadas, two white-fronts and 20 light geese. With the Delta still parched, the Game and Fish Commission on Thursday pointed hunters to its weekly Wetland Management updates at agfc.com/wetlandmanagement, which show water levels and food on public flooded timber and moist-soil units. The youth and veteran waterfowl hunts are Feb. 6-7.'))
body.append(P('<b>Small game and what is ahead.</b> Quail is open statewide through Feb. 7 (daily limit six), and AGFC listings for wildlife management areas such as Big Lake show squirrel (limit 12) and rabbit (limit eight) open through Feb. 28; check agfc.com for private-land rules. The Commission is taking public comment, through a survey at agfc.com, on a proposal to move the 2027 spring turkey season about a week earlier.'))
body.append('<h2>Postscript</h2>')
body.append('<p class="ps">“Be through my lips to unawaken’d earth<br>The trumpet of a prophecy! O Wind,<br>If Winter comes, can Spring be far behind?”</p>')
body.append('<p class="ps">&mdash; Percy Bysshe Shelley, “Ode to the West Wind” (1820). With two summery days left before Sunday’s front ushers in the first hard cold, Shelley’s famous closing question is a good one to carry into the season.</p>')
body.append('<p class="sign">Until tomorrow, with good wishes &mdash;<br><b>The Editor</b></p>')
body.append('<div class="foot">Compiled from many reports.</div>')



# Pastimes
import os, json as _json
fleet = _json.load(open('fleet.json')) if os.path.exists('fleet.json') else None
def fleet_svg(d):
    n=d['size']; cs=30; lab=18; W=lab+n*cs; H=lab+n*cs
    o=[f'<svg viewBox="0 0 {W} {H}" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="width:100%;height:auto;display:block;font-family:inherit">']
    for c in range(n): o.append(f'<text x="{lab+c*cs+cs/2}" y="12" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" fill="#8a6a35">{chr(65+c)}</text>')
    for r in range(n):
        o.append(f'<text x="{lab/2}" y="{lab+r*cs+cs/2+4}" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" fill="#4a3618">{r+1}</text>')
        for c in range(n):
            x=lab+c*cs;y=lab+r*cs
            o.append(f'<rect x="{x}" y="{y}" width="{cs}" height="{cs}" fill="none" stroke="#8a6a35" stroke-width="1"/><text x="{x+cs/2}" y="{y+cs/2+6}" text-anchor="middle" font-size="17" font-weight="bold" fill="#2b2118">{d["grid"][r][c]}</text>')
    o.append('</svg>'); return ''.join(o)
body.append('<div class="page pastimes"><h2>The Pastimes</h2>')
left='<div><h3>I. Mate in Two</h3>'+f'<div class="board">{past["chess_svg"]}</div>'+f'<p>{past["chess_prompt"]} White is shown with the lighter, outlined pieces; the board is oriented with White at the bottom.</p></div>'
if fleet:
    tot=sum(len(x['word']) for x in fleet['ships'])
    clues=''.join(f'<li><span class="bl">{" ".join("_" for _ in x["word"])}</span> ({len(x["word"])}) {x["hint"]}</li>' for x in fleet['ships'])
    right=('<div><h3>II. Word Fleet</h3>'+'<div style="width:84%">'+fleet_svg(fleet)+'</div>'+
      f'<ol class="clues fl">{clues}</ol>'
      f'<p class="note">Each clue is a definition. Find its word in the grid, across or down. Fire by putting a pencil dot on a letter: one dot is one shot. Check the answers below, then circle each right letter and cross out each wrong one. Your score is your number of dots. <b>Best possible score: {tot} shots.</b> My shots: ________</p></div>')
    sol_ii='II. '+', '.join(f"{x['word']} {chr(65+x['c'])}{x['r']+1} {'across' if x['dir']=='A' else 'down'}" for x in fleet['ships'])
else:
    right='<div><h3>II. The Four Houses</h3>'+f'<p>{past["logic_intro"]}</p><ol class="clues">'+''.join(f'<li>{c}</li>' for c in past['logic_clues'])+f'</ol><p>{past["logic_question"]}</p></div>'
    sol_ii='II. '+past['logic_answer']
body.append('<div class="two">'+left+right+'</div>')
if os.path.exists('comic/today.svg'):
    body.append('<h3 style="margin-top:2pt;margin-bottom:0">III. Sorry, Earth</h3><div class="comic" style="margin:1pt 0 0 0"><img src="comic/today.png" style="width:92%;display:block;margin:0 auto"></div>')
body.append('<div class="flip box"><b>Solutions printed upside down.</b><br>I. '+past['chess_solution']+'<br>'+sol_ii+'</div></div>')

html = f'<!doctype html><html><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>The Morning Letter</title><style>{css}</style></head><body>'+'\n'.join(body)+'</body></html>'
open('letter.html','w').write(html)
print(len(html))

